/-!
# A plane curve is a curve exactly when its equation is irreducible

Let `k` be a field and let `Z ⊆ 𝐏²_k = Proj k[T₀, T₁, T₂]` be a closed subscheme of dimension one
whose homogeneous ideal is generated by a single polynomial, `I(Z) = (F)`. This file proves
**Tag 0BYC**: `Z` is a curve over `k` — an integral scheme, separated and of finite type over `k`,
of dimension one — if and only if `F` is irreducible.

Both directions pass through the identification `Z ≅ Proj (k[T₀, T₁, T₂] ⧸ I(Z))` over `𝐏²_k`,
which is `Stacks.ClosedSubschemeProjQuotient.SaturationIdentification`. If `F` is irreducible
then `(F)` is prime, the quotient is a graded domain, and `Proj` of a graded domain is integral.
Conversely if `Z` is integral then so is `Proj` of the quotient, and the homogeneous ideal of the
whole of an integral `Proj` is prime; pulling that back along the quotient map shows `(F)` prime,
hence `F` irreducible.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpectrum.Proj.projectivePlane_isCurve_iff_irreducible`:
  **Tag 0BYC**, the equivalence.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpectrum.Proj`
  `.projectivePlane_isCurve_iff_irreducible_of_equidimensional`: the same under the
  equidimensionality hypothesis of Tag 0BYB, rather than under the dimension of `Z`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpectrum.Proj.projectivePlane_isPrime_span_singleton_of_isCurve`
  and `AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpectrum.Proj`
  `.projectivePlane_ne_zero_of_topologicalKrullDim_eq_one`:
  the two halves the equivalence is assembled from — the equation of a plane curve generates a
  prime ideal, and the equation of a one-dimensional closed subscheme is nonzero.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpectrum.Proj.projectivePlane_isVariety_of_isIntegral`: an integral
  closed subscheme of `𝐏²_k` is a variety over `k`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpectrum.Proj`
  `.projectiveSpace_idealOfClosedSubscheme_ne_top`: the homogeneous ideal of a nonempty closed
  subscheme of projective space is proper. This is what rules out a unit as the equation of a
  plane curve, hence what makes its degree positive.

## Implementation notes

The source's hypotheses are those of [Stacks, Tag 0BYB] — `Z` equidimensional of dimension one
with no embedded points — but the equivalence needs neither the absence of embedded points nor
the equidimensionality, only that `dim Z = 1`, and it is stated with that single hypothesis. For a
nonempty `Z` satisfying the hypotheses of Tag 0BYB this is
`topologicalKrullDim_le_of_forall_mem_irreducibleComponents` together with the dimension of any
one component, so the statement below applies to every `Z` as in Tag 0BYB; the hypothesis is used
both to produce a point of `Z` and to rule out `F = 0`, which would make `Z` the whole plane.

"Curve" is `AlgebraicGeometry.IsCurve` of [Stacks, Tag 0A23], a variety of Krull dimension one,
taken with respect to the structure morphism
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjPolyChart.projToSpecCoeff k 2` of the polynomial model of the
projective plane. Its variety clauses come for free from the closed immersion: separatedness and
quasi-compactness from those of `Proj k[T₀, T₁, T₂] ⟶ Spec k`, and finite type from its
smoothness, `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjPolyChart.smooth_projToSpecCoeff`. So the only clause
with content is integrality of `Z`, and that is what the equivalence trades for irreducibility
of `F`.

Five auxiliary facts are proved privately, none of them special to the plane. Three concern
`Proj` of a graded ring: that `Proj` of a graded domain is integral, that the homogeneous ideal of
all of `Proj 𝒜` is prime when `Proj 𝒜` is irreducible, and that an element vanishing at every
point of a reduced `Proj 𝒜` is killed by a power of any degree-one element. The last of these is
what turns vanishing on all of `Proj` into divisibility by `F`, and it is applied at two of the
three homogeneous coordinates: the two resulting divisibilities `F ∣ T₀ⁿ a` and `F ∣ T₁ᵐ a` give
`F ∣ a` because `T₀` and `T₁` are coprime in the polynomial ring.

The remaining two concern a closed subscheme `Z` of `Proj R[T₀, …, T_{e-1}]` over a Noetherian
ring: that it is `Proj` of the quotient by its homogeneous ideal, and that it is the whole space
when that ideal is zero. Both come from
`Stacks.ProjQuotientChartIdeal.saturationIdentification_of_app_awayMk_eq_ideal` and
`Stacks.ProjQuotientChartIdeal.ker_le_ker_of_app_awayMk`, which reduce a comparison of ideal
sheaves to a comparison of degree-zero fractions on the standard charts, fed with the chart
computation `AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpectrum.Proj.mem_ker_app_basicOpen_iff`; the covering
family is the homogeneous coordinates, and only the exponent bookkeeping between the two — one
indexing an equation by `(j + n) • 1` and the other a fraction by `n` — needs doing, by
`chartFraction_eq_of_cross`.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0BYC](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BYC)
